														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1970062">
									

<p>The reason they don't do any research is because the containment procedures prohibit it. They don't know <em>why</em> the containment procedures say that, because the documentation was lost in the mentioned unrelated breach, but they trust that they must have had a good reason to prohibit it.</p>
<p>There'd be researchers in the Foundation coming up with reasons why they should reopen research, but the higher ups will ask: why? We have it safely contained, at minimal cost, and we gain little and risk a lot by breaching the procedures we must have had a very good reason to put in place.</p>
<p>Remember also that the only reason we know that the skip is potentially non-existent or neutralised is the document that they can't even view (our peeking at it by overriding the seal is a necessary OOC thing&#8230;within the SCP universe you could expect a bullet in the back of your head from the guard alerted to the containment breach of you reading the document. Imagine that as a headfuck actually: &quot;Oh my god, this SCP doesn't exist! I've got to tell ever-&quot; BLAM!) ostensively written by a D-Class; it may not be accurate, it might not even have been written by a D-Class, it might even be that reading it infects you, as they suspect it might.</p>
<p>The point is, they don't know, so they err on the side of caution. Would you want to be the one to risk it?</p>

							</div>

								
